17aa34e524aa6c5f5dca293a16bffa77782016c3fd752bcfa4b0a", "bytes": 293019, "lines": 637, "events_no_deltas": [{"type": "system", "subtype": "init", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tools": ["StructuredOutput"], "mcp_servers": [], "permissionMode": "auto", "claude_code_version": "2.1.294", "output_style": "default", "init_sha256": "19a6ffcf0781096cd8fadcc901d1f698c81748761870f4f438dc6f07a56b3d9a"}, {"type": "system", "subtype": "status", "status": "requesting"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "message_start", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgSvPg1zbxVRCp2xi9gkT", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7216, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7216}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "ttft_ms": 1971, "thinking_display": "updates"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 0, "content_block": {"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "thinking_display": "updates"}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgSvPg1zbxVRCp2xi9gkT", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": {"длина": 62568, "sha256": "813ff0626dbbdbb75dcbc64c2e571c6d46392e68d24f68b9b815f4571b513f28"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7216, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7216}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-27T14:48:57.030Z", "request_id": "req_011CgSvPfkNuY4NxLxM1tS9p", "thinking_duration_ms": 241801}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgSvPg1zbxVRCp2xi9gkT", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I pick **candidate 2**, with only about 35% confidence. Six candidates order the same way and their estimates sit within roughly 200 of each other, so a single random run can easily change the order. I did the estimates in my head with a rough random-walk approximation of stock over time, so treat every figure as approximate.\n\n**Checking the plans against the rules**\n- All plans time their orders to the delivery lags: product 0 ordered on day t matches the forecast for day t+2, product 1 for day t+4.\n- No plan can avoid the early shortages. Product 0 starts with 45 units against about 52 of demand on days 0–1, roughly 11 units short. Product 1 starts with 45 against about 120 of demand on days 0–3, roughly 75 units short.\n- Spoilage barely matters. A product 0 batch only spoils if stock exceeds about 4 days of demand (about 135 units), which is rare. The 240-unit warehouse limit is reached nowhere except near candidate 6's big batches.\n\n**The trade-off**\n- Each unit of demand lost to a shortage costs 18 for product 0 and 16 for product 1 (lost revenue plus the penalty).\n- Each surplus unit costs its purchase price (7 or 5) plus 1 or 0.9 for every night it is held.\n- Because the plan ignores actual demand, stock behaves like a random walk floored at zero. Its spread grows over time, to about ±60 units by the end.\n\n**Candidate by candidate**\n- **Candidate 4 (×0.95):** about 115 lost units of product 0 and 88 of product 1. It is about 240 worse than candidate 0.\n- **Candidate 0 (×1.0):** demand runs about 4.6% above supply, giving about 78 lost units of product 0 and 60 of product 1. Holding cost is moderate.\n- **Candidate 1 (×1.1) and candidate 5 (×1.15):** shortages fall, but the surplus builds up and is held for many nights. Holding cost rises faster than the shortage savings, mostly for product 1. Candidate 1 comes out about 200 worse than candidate 0, and candidate 5 worse still.\n- **Candidate 3 (×1.05 plus a buffer of 10):** about equal to candidate 0.\n- **Candidate 2 (×1.0 plus a buffer of 25 in the first order, last orders trimmed):** the buffer arrives exactly when stock is at zero and shortages are most likely, so it is the cheapest form of safety stock.\n  - Product 0: about 21 fewer lost units, worth about +380, against −175 for purchase and about −130 for holding, a net of about +70.\n  - Product 1: about 20 fewer lost units, worth about +320, against −125 for purchase and about −110 for holding, a net of about +90.\n  - Trimming the last orders is roughly neutral to slightly positive.\n  - Overall it comes out about 170–200 better than candidates 0 and 3.\n- **Candidates 7 and 6 (ordering every 2 or 3 days):** they save 308 and 396 in fixed fees. However, each delivery covers 2–3 days, so the extra stock held overnight costs about 850 for candidate 7 and about 1,600 for candidate 6. Both are clearly worse.\n\nThe comparison with candidate 0 is fairly robust, because the buffer pays off whenever cumulative shortages reach about 25, which happens in most scenarios. The gap to candidates 3 and 1 depends much more on the actual random demand."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7216, "cac